1069 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 254 0 R
/E (As a standalone product, crop monitoring struggles to demonstrate direct economic value: detecting a disease only improves outcomes if farmers can access an appropriate and affordable response. Without integration into advisory, input distribution, or cooperative infrastructure, detection capability translates poorly into farm-level impact. Most viable approaches integrate monitoring into broader service offerings where it contributes indirectly to value creation by improving decision quality or reducing risk.)
/Pg 43 0 R
/K [10]
>>
endobj
1070 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /P
/P 254 0 R
/E (Recent LLM and generative AI advances have expanded access to image-based diagnostics. In African agricultural contexts, their practical value remains limited by underrepresentation of local crops and conditions in training data, and by absence of integration with agricultural service ecosystems. These tools may complement specialized solutions in future but do not currently substitute for locally grounded calibration.)
/Pg 43 0 R
/K [11]
>>
endobj
